cyrenit - a minimalist init system and supervisor
=================================================

cyrenit is a small experimental init program written in C. It provides a
tiny and simple process supervisor to start a set of services. It intends
to provide the basic infrastructure for an init (PID 1) program and have
a simple foundation for exploring post-boot semantics for the linux kernel.


WARNING: This is not intended for production, and this project is more of
a playground for exerimenting with init design and service launching,
signal handling, etc. It runs on a constrained initcpio image for now.

1. REQUIREMENTS
To build cyrenit the requirements are:
- GNU make
- A C compiler (tested on gcc)
- A linker (tested on ld)
- bash

To build an initcpio image you will need:
- cpio
- bash
- tools: ls, find, mount, umount, df, cp, mv, rm, dmesg, touch, cat,
  tail, ln, ps and kill (you'll likely have it, but might remove them
  from the Makefile IMAGE_BINS list as well if you don't have and don't
  want them)

To run cyrenit you will need:
- QEMU
- A kernel at your host's /boot/vmlinuz-linux (you can change the path on
  the Makefile)

2. BUILDING
If you hadn't generated autoconf, run:
To build cyrenit run:
$ ./configure
$ make

To build the initcpio run:
$ make initcpio

2.1 Config Flags
The `./configure` script can be built with several optional flags. Those
flags will set the value of config variables, named following the
pattern `CONFIG_VARNAME`, while the flags are named `--enable-this`,
`--disable-that`, `--with-this=value`, etc, pretty standard autoconf
ways.

2.2 Make Vars
Some make variables (passed as `VAR=value` to `make`) control some
behaviors of the building and running process. They will often be
toggle variables, meaning, if you set them to 1 you enable them,
otherwise they are disabled.

For `make run`, the following variables are relevant:
 KERN_CONS - when set to 1, enables a kernel console more verbose output
 by passing `debug` to the kernel cmdline. When not set ot set to other
 value, it passes `quiet` to the kernel cmdline making kernel only output
 errors and warnings.

 LOGLEVEL - Sets the kernel loglevel, defaulting to 3, or to 7 if
 KERN_CONS is set to 1.

 QEMU_DEBUG - when set to 1, starts qemu on debug mode, waiting for gdb
 to connect on port 1234.

3. RUNNING
To run cyrenit, use the command:
$ make run

4. DEBUGGING
To build cyrenit with debug symbols, use `--enable-debug` argument to
`./configure`.
You can debug the CLI mode just running it regularly with `gdb`.

To debug it on PID1 mode, build it with debug symbols and then add the
`QEMU_DEBUG=1` argument to `make run` as well. Qemu will then start and
listen on port 1234 so you can connect to it using gdb command:

`target remote :1234`

Then you start the execution with `continue`, although I'd recommend to
add some breakpoints before (i.e. `main`).

